{French Defense: Tarrasch Variation. Book so far. The plan is the usual one: finish development, then decide which side to open.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Be7 5. Nf3 Nf6 {A slight inaccuracy in move order — the bishop is better placed before this pawn commits.} 6. Nxf6+ Bxf6 7. Bd3 c5 8. dxc5 ! Nd7 {The critical moment. The pieces are as good as they are going to get; something has to happen now.} (8... e5 Rb1 e4 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. Be3 Bxb2 10. Rb1 Qa5+ 11. Kf1 Qxa2 ?! 12. h4 Bf6 13. Be4 O-O 14. g4 Qc4+ 15. Bd3 Qxg4 16. Rg1 Qh3+ 17. Ke2 e5 18. Rb4 Rd8 19. Rh1 Qe6 20. Ng5 Bxg5 21. Bxh7+ Kxh7 22. hxg5+ Kg8 23. Rbh4 Kf8 24. Rh8+ Ke7 25. Qd6+ Qxd6 26. cxd6+ Kxd6 27. Rxd8 Kc7 28. Rhh8 b6 29. Ke1 a5 30. Bc1 a4 31. Ba3 b5 32. c3 Rb8 33. Bb4 Ra8 34. Rdg8 a3 35. Bxa3 Rxa3 36. Rxc8+ Kd6 37. Rhg8 f6 38. g6 Ra1+ 39. Ke2 Ra2+ 40. Kf3 Nb6 41. Rb8 Nd5 42. Rxg7 Ke6 43. Rgb7 Ra3 44. g7 Rxc3+ 45. Kg4 f5+ 46. Kg5 Rc1 47. g8=Q+ Kd6 48. Rd8+ Kc5 49. Qxd5+ Kb4 50. Rxb5+ Kc3 {Precise to the end. A pawn move here instead would have let the defence build a fortress.} 51. Rc8# 1-0
